  "answer_text": "When receiving food deliveries in a commercial kitchen or food service establishment, food safety guidelines (such as the FDA Food Code and ServSafe) require different types of food to be accepted at specific temperatures:\n\n### 1. Cold TCS (Time/Temperature Control for Safety) Food\n* **Accepting Temperature:** **41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower**.\n* **Examples:** Raw meat, poultry, seafood, dairy, and pre-cut produce. \n* **Action:** Reject any cold TCS food that arrives above 41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C), as it has entered the \"Temperature Danger Zone\" where pathogens can rapidly grow.\n\n### 2. Hot TCS Food\n* **Accepting Temperature:** **135\u00b0F (57\u00b0C) or higher**.\n* **Examples:** Pre-cooked hot dishes, soups, or rotisserie items being delivered hot.\n* **Action:** Reject hot food if it falls below 135\u00b0F (57\u00b0C).\n\n### 3. Frozen Food\n* **Accepting Temperature:** Must be received **frozen solid**.\n* **Action:** Reject frozen food if there are signs of thawing and refreezing, such as:\n  * Large ice crystals on the food or packaging.\n  * Fluids, water stains, or dampness on the bottom of the cases.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Special Exceptions (The \"45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C)\" Rule)\nA few specific high-risk foods are permitted by the FDA to be accepted at slightly higher temperatures, provided they are cooled quickly after receipt:\n\n* **Milk:** Can be received at **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C) or lower**, but must be cooled to 41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower within **4 hours**.\n* **Shell Eggs:** Can be received at an air temperature of **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C) or lower**.\n* **Live Shellfish (Oysters, Mussels, Clams, Scallops):** Can be received at an air temperature of **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C)** and an internal temperature no greater than **50\u00b0F (10\u00b0C)**. Once received, they must be cooled to 41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower within **4 hours**.\n* **Shucked Shellfish:** Can be received at **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C) or lower**, but must be cooled to 41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower within **4 hours**.\n\n---\n\n### Quick Guide on How to Check Receiving Temperatures:\n* **Meat, Poultry, or Fish:** Insert a clean, calibrated thermometer probe directly into the thickest part of the food.\n* **ROP (Reduced Oxygen Packaging / Vacuum-Sealed) Food:** Place the thermometer probe *between* two packages. **Do not** puncture the packaging.\n* **Other Packaged Foods (like milk cartons):** Open one package and insert the probe into the food, ensuring the probe does not touch the packaging itself.",
